    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Attribution: link to <code>https://otakumanifesto.org</code> and name the author.
    That is enough.
  </p>

  <h2>Contributing</h2>

  <p>
    The source is public at
    <a href="https://github.com/esdras-schonevald/otakumanifesto.org">github.com/esdras-schonevald/otakumanifesto.org</a>.
    The site is a static Astro build — no runtime, no database. Translations live in
    <code>src/i18n/</code>; the rules are data, so every language renders the same
    table from the same source of truth.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Corrections</strong> (typos, broken links, wrong code) — open an issue or a PR directly.</li>
    <li><strong>New examples</strong> in another language — welcome, provided the rule is unchanged.</li>
    <li><strong>Changing a rule</strong> — open an issue first. The rules are intentionally hard to change; that is the point.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Versioning</h2>

  <p>
    The philosophy is versioned by date, not by semantic version. A rule is never
    silently edited: changes are announced, discussed and reflected in the guide with
    an example of what changed.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Version</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>2026.10</td><td>Second edition: forty measurable rules, cyclomatic complexity as the primary metric, TypeScript, and translations into six languages.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2026.10</td><td>First public edition outside PHPRise: five pillars, three shapes, cross-language guide.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>—</td><td>Earlier internal drafts lived inside PHPRise repositories.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <Note label="A note on zeal">
    <p>
      A philosophy that turns into a personality test has failed. The rules are about
      code, and code can be changed. Argue with the rule, never with the person.
    </p>
  </Note>
</Doc>
